  "content": {
    "id": "p60-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "Find the equation of the normal to the curve $y=(3x-4)^3$ at $(1,-1)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x+9y+8=0$",
      "worked": "Using the chain rule,\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}&=3(3x-4)^2\\times3\\\\&=9(3x-4)^2.\\end{aligned}$$\nAt $x=1$, the tangent gradient is\n$$m=9(3-4)^2=9.$$\nThe normal gradient is\n$$m_{\\perp}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$$\nUsing $(1,-1)$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-(-1)&=-\\frac19(x-1)\\\\9y+9&=-x+1\\\\x+9y+8&=0.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
